        "nodes": [
          {
            "id": "h-11a6bd07-10dc-58c3-90ae-ce276f0752fc",
            "kind": "task",
            "label": "Marshall management appears to have contained the O-ring concern within the project instead of communicating it to Levels I and II (the Commission's Finding 3 is worded 'appears to be a propensity').",
            "description": "Hypothesis · Supported · 6 supporting, 3 contradicting evidence"
          },
          {
            "id": "h-46f246bd-e0c1-5175-99b0-045a41ff7d92",
            "kind": "task",
            "label": "Thiokol management reversed its engineers' no-launch recommendation at the urging of Marshall to accommodate a major customer.",
            "description": "Hypothesis · Supported · 6 supporting, 1 contradicting evidence"
          },
          {
            "id": "h-4978f2e3-b3cd-5562-8bd6-398f0812d98d",
            "kind": "task",
            "label": "The faulty design of the right Solid Rocket Motor aft field joint caused the pressure seal to fail, letting combustion gas leak and weaken and/or penetrate the External Tank.",
            "description": "Hypothesis · Supported · 12 supporting, 0 contradicting evidence"
          },
          {
            "id": "h-5635d5c6-82d5-5809-bd2b-2b2b315c9766",
            "kind": "task",
            "label": "The launch decision process was flawed: Thiokol's initial recommendation against launch and the continuing opposition of its engineers did not reach those who made the final decision, and the O-ring erosion history, although briefed to Level I in August 1985, was not acted on as a constraint on launch.",
            "description": "Hypothesis · Supported · 6 supporting, 0 contradicting evidence"
          },
          {
            "id": "h-805ac71e-6a26-5042-81be-e1ccda84e85a",
            "kind": "task",
            "label": "At ignition the O-rings, their resiliency reduced by cold (modelled at 28 +/- 5 degrees F at the coldest point of the joint), combined with a tight tang-to-clevis gap and strut-enhanced joint opening near the 300-degree position, probably could not follow the opening gap quickly enough to seal, so blow-by and erosion began at or shortly after ignition.",
            "description": "Hypothesis · Supported · 11 supporting, 0 contradicting evidence"
          },
          {
            "id": "h-b00a6002-4ce5-5fdd-a03a-e362f3007dce",
            "kind": "task",
            "label": "The safety, reliability and quality assurance organization was silent: absent from the launch-decision forums and doing little or no trend analysis of O-ring distress.",
            "description": "Hypothesis · Supported · 2 supporting, 0 contradicting evidence"
          },
          {
            "id": "h-ddd2415c-1f5c-5e25-8b0d-f15117282721",
            "kind": "task",
            "label": "Over the preceding years NASA and Thiokol accepted escalating O-ring erosion and blow-by as an acceptable flight risk, apparently because earlier flights had survived it, waiving launch constraints and relying on flight-readiness rationales (later literature calls this normalization; the 1986 sources say 'acceptable risk').",
            "description": "Hypothesis · Supported · 12 supporting, 0 contradicting evidence"
          },
          {
            "id": "h-ea8ba6ca-3918-5c8b-97c8-e900d02bf1c7",
            "kind": "task",
            "label": "Pressure to meet an ambitious flight rate left no margin for unforeseen hardware problems and may have obscured engineering concerns (Rogers hedges this: 'may occasionally'; the House Committee finds it directly contributed to unsafe launch operations).",
            "description": "Hypothesis · Supported · 3 supporting, 0 contradicting evidence"
          },
          {
            "id": "c-663ba4a6-2a30-56b7-ae39-e3d04c8d4f78",
            "kind": "end",
            "label": "Loss of Challenger 73 seconds after launch on 28 January 1986",
            "description": "Outcome · causal relationship Confirmed"
          }
